g or an individual assessment. The CDC recommends talking with your child’s doctor about missed milestones, lost skills, or other concerns.\n\n## Sources & editorial notes\n\nPublished by CradleNote. Competitor information comes from these official sources, not a hands-on review. Product names belong to their respective owners; no affiliation or endorsement is implied.\n\nMeet CradleNote\n\n## One place for the people who care.\n\nShare the day’s care, find activities to try together, and keep the memories your family makes along the way.\n\nDownload for iPhoneUse the web app\n\nCurrently free to download and use. Account required. iPhone app requires iOS 17 or later.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThis is Little One App for iPhone and iPad | appstor.io (https://little-one-chdays.appstor.io/)\nciteturn86search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; \n\nAbout\n\n## About this app\n\nLittle Ones Stories are the childrens books where the child you read to is the main character. In this free story you can try it out and read about how the child paints a self portrait and more paintings. Try out our other longer stories where you can make more personal choices and an adult gets to join In the book!\n\nLittle One’s Stories is a collection of apps for parents and children. The idea is to take the classic children’s book to the next level with the possibility to edit the main character to make it look like the child it is read to. There are also a few decisions to make in each book.\n\nReading together with small children is a treasured tradition we would love to see go on. And with today’s children spending more and more time in front of a screen, why not turn that to an opportunity and go back to basics?\n\nThis app is made for children and is developed by parents. In order for parents to feel secure that the child will not be tricked into buying anything or being linked to any web sites, this and any future app will not h